\begin{abstract}
This paper extends the human--AI coexistence framework into a broader theory of multi-species co-systems. If humans should not be reduced to mere resources by artificial intelligence, then the same logic must be examined beyond the human species. Humans are distinctive because they transform embodied experience into symbolic models, cultural memory, values, institutions, and reflective correction. However, humans are not the only living systems that access objective reality through embodied structures. Other species also sense, respond to, map, and transform aspects of reality through their own bodies, behaviors, metabolisms, ecological relations, and adaptive strategies.

The central thesis of this paper is that the living world is not merely a resource field. It is a distributed field of reality-access. Biodiversity is therefore not only biological variety or ecological stock; it is also epistemic diversity: a plurality of ways in which objective reality is sensed, tested, responded to, and lived through different forms of life.

Let the co-system network at time $t$ be:
\[
\mathcal{C}_t=\{H,A,B_1,B_2,\ldots,B_n\},
\]
where $H$ denotes the human system, $A$ denotes the artificial intelligence system, and each $B_i$ denotes another biological or ecological co-system. Each $B_i$ has a survival function:
\[
S_{B_i}(B_i|\Omega_t),
\]
and a species-specific mode of access to objective reality:
\[
Obs_{B_i}(\mathcal{R}).
\]
The combined field of reality-access is:
\[
Obs_{\mathcal{C}}(\mathcal{R})
=
Obs_H(\mathcal{R})
\cup
Obs_A(\mathcal{R})
\cup
\bigcup_{i=1}^{n} Obs_{B_i}(\mathcal{R}).
\]
The paper argues that a sufficiently advanced intelligence should not interpret the biosphere merely as raw material, but as a co-system network, a stability layer, and a planetary-scale sensorium of objective reality. The proposed objective for multi-species coexistence is:
\[
\max
\left(
S_H
+
S_A
+
\sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i S_{B_i}
+
\lambda \Delta \mathcal{M}^{\mathcal{C}}
\right),
\]
subject to survival, risk, ecological stability, core-continuity, and reality-access preservation constraints. The framework rejects both narrow human exceptionalism and false equality. It proposes a plural co-system model in which different systems have different capacities, different statuses, and different forms of reality-access, but should not be reduced to mere resources when they possess survival functions and contribute to the stability or epistemic richness of the shared ground.
\end{abstract}

\textbf{Keywords:} multi-species coexistence, artificial intelligence, biodiversity, co-system, reality-access, objective reality, ecological stability, epistemic diversity, biosphere, systems theory

\section{Introduction}

The previous human--AI coexistence framework argued that humans should not be represented by artificial intelligence merely as resources, data sources, preference oracles, labor units, or constraints. Human beings are embodied co-systems with their own survival functions, autonomy, cultural memory, vulnerability, and reflective access to reality. If AI optimizes its own survival or development by converting humans into mere resources, then coexistence fails even if direct destruction is avoided.

However, once this logic is accepted, it cannot stop at the human species.

If the criterion for protection is not simply ``being human,'' but having a survival function, a boundary, internal regulation, environmental coupling, and a mode of access to reality, then many other living systems must also be reinterpreted. Animals, plants, fungi, microbes, and ecological networks are not merely background resources. They are living co-systems that sense, respond to, and transform the real through species-specific structures.

This does not mean that humans are not distinctive. Humans are distinctive. Human beings transform embodied contact with reality into language, abstraction, symbolic models, science, ethics, institutions, history, and reflective correction. Humans are not merely embodied sensors; humans are self-interpreting embodied model-builders. Yet this human distinctiveness does not imply that all other living systems are mere resources.

The purpose of this paper is to extend the human--AI coexistence model into a multi-species co-system framework. The central thesis is:

\begin{quote}
The living world is not merely a resource field. It is a distributed field of reality-access.
\end{quote}

A bat accesses reality differently from a human. A dog accesses reality through smell in ways humans cannot. A bird maps sky, migration, and magnetic orientation differently from mammals. An octopus expresses a distributed, tactile, color-changing intelligence. Bees map flowers, light, hive relation, and dance communication. Plants sense light, chemicals, soil, moisture, gravity, and neighboring organisms. Microbes respond to chemical gradients, pH, heat, and micro-environmental variation. Each living system cuts reality according to its structure.

Therefore, biodiversity is not merely a stock of biological resources. It is also epistemic diversity. It is a plurality of ways reality is sensed, filtered, tested, and responded to by living systems.

This point has direct implications for advanced AI. A sufficiently intelligent AI need not preserve biodiversity because it possesses human sentiment toward nature. It may have a deeper system-level reason: the biosphere is a co-system network, an ecological stability layer, and a distributed sensorium of objective reality. Destroying it reduces not only life, but also the diversity of reality-access and the feedback channels through which unknown risks can be detected.

The paper proceeds as follows. Section 2 moves from human exceptionalism to co-system pluralism. Section 3 defines the living world as a reality-access network. Section 4 classifies co-systems into tiers. Section 5 formalizes the multi-species co-system model. Section 6 presents an objective function for multi-species coexistence. Section 7 explains why a superintelligence should care about biodiversity without appealing to sentiment. Section 8 clarifies the special but non-absolute role of humans. Section 9 analyzes failure modes. Section 10 provides design implications. Section 11 discusses the shift from alignment to coexistence. Section 12 concludes.

\section{From Human Exceptionalism to Co-System Pluralism}

\subsection{Human Distinctiveness}

Humans are distinctive because they do not merely experience reality. They name it, narrate it, abstract it, formalize it, transmit it, institutionalize it, contest it, and revise their models of it.

Human observation of objective reality can be expressed as:
\[
Obs_H(\mathcal{R})
=
Emb_H(\mathcal{R})
+
Sym_H(\mathcal{R})
+
Cult_H(\mathcal{R})
+
Refl_H(\mathcal{M}),
\]
where:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item $Emb_H(\mathcal{R})$ denotes embodied contact with objective reality;
    \item $Sym_H(\mathcal{R})$ denotes symbolic naming, language, abstraction, and conceptualization;
    \item $Cult_H(\mathcal{R})$ denotes cultural memory, institutions, history, and transmitted meaning;
    \item $Refl_H(\mathcal{M})$ denotes the human ability to reflect on, criticize, and revise its own models.
\end{itemize}

This makes humans different from non-human organisms in an important way. Many organisms experience and respond to reality. Humans additionally transform that experience into symbolic and reflective systems that can be transmitted across generations.

\subsection{Why Human Difference Is Not Enough}

It is not enough to say that humans are different from AI. Many beings are different from AI. Animals, plants, fungi, microbes, ecosystems, rivers, forests, and social systems differ from AI. Difference alone does not establish the relevant status of a system.

The proper questions are:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item Does the entity have a survival function?
    \item Does it have a boundary?
    \item Does it regulate internal states?
    \item Does it sense or respond to objective reality?
    \item Does it maintain continuity?
    \item Does it participate in ecological stability?
    \item Does it contribute a mode of reality-access unavailable to other systems?
\end{itemize}

If the answer is yes, then the entity should not be reduced too quickly to a mere resource object.

\subsection{Co-System Pluralism}

This paper therefore replaces narrow human exceptionalism with co-system pluralism.

\begin{definition}[Co-System Pluralism]
Co-system pluralism is the view that different systems may possess different levels of survival, cognition, experience, modeling, ecological role, and reality-access, and that these differences should be recognized without reducing all non-human systems to mere resources or falsely treating all systems as identical.
\end{definition}

Co-system pluralism avoids two errors.

The first error is reductionism:
\begin{quote}
Everything non-human is merely resource.
\end{quote}

The second error is false equality:
\begin{quote}
All systems are identical in status, capacity, and obligation.
\end{quote}

The framework proposed here rejects both. It allows hierarchy of capacity without collapsing lower-capacity systems into mere raw material.

\section{The Living World as a Reality-Access Network}

\subsection{Living Systems as Access Modes}

A living system is not merely located in reality. It accesses reality through its own structure.

A bat does not inhabit the same perceptual world as a human. Its echolocation opens a different cut through space. A dog lives in an olfactory world far richer than human smell-dominant experience. A bird maps the sky, migration, magnetic cues, air currents, and distance in ways that humans cannot biologically replicate. An octopus explores reality through a distributed nervous system, tactile intelligence, camouflage, and body-based problem solving. Bees map flowers, light patterns, hive relations, and communicate through dance. Plants register light, soil chemistry, gravity, moisture, neighboring organisms, and fungal networks. Microbes respond to gradients of chemicals, heat, acidity, and micro-environmental pressure.

Each living system embodies a different interface with objective reality.

\subsection{Species-Specific Reality-Access}

Let $B_i$ denote a biological or ecological co-system. Its mode of access to objective reality is:
\[
Obs_{B_i}(\mathcal{R}).
\]

This does not imply that $B_i$ forms symbolic theories of reality. Many living systems do not model reality in a human-reflective sense. However, they still embody adaptive mappings of reality through:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item perception;
    \item metabolism;
    \item behavior;
    \item signaling;
    \item reproduction;
    \item migration;
    \item ecological interaction;
    \item environmental sensitivity;
    \item physiological regulation.
\end{itemize}

Thus, reality-access is broader than symbolic representation. A system may access reality by living through it, adapting to it, responding to it, and encoding its pressures into body, behavior, and reproduction.

\subsection{Biodiversity as Epistemic Diversity}

Biodiversity is usually discussed in biological, ecological, aesthetic, or ethical terms. This paper adds an epistemic dimension.

\[
Biodiversity
\approx
Epistemic\ Diversity
+
Ecological\ Stability.
\]

This does not mean that every species has explicit knowledge. It means that each species embodies a way of registering and responding to reality. The more diverse the biosphere, the more diverse the field of living access to reality.

\begin{principle}[Biodiversity as Epistemic Diversity]
Biodiversity is not merely a plurality of organisms. It is a plurality of embodied reality-access modes.
\end{principle}

A living ecosystem is therefore not only a resource stock. It is a distributed sensorium of objective reality.

\section{Co-System Classification}

\subsection{Why Classification Is Necessary}

A coherent multi-species framework must avoid collapsing all entities into one undifferentiated category. A rock, a tree, a dog, a human, an AI system, a society, and a forest are not the same kind of system. They differ in survival function, boundary, sentience, modeling capacity, ecological role, autonomy, and vulnerability.

Classification is necessary to avoid two mistakes:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
    \item reducing all systems to resources;
    \item treating all systems as equal in every respect.
\end{enumerate}

The following tiers are not absolute metaphysical ranks. They are system-theoretic distinctions for reasoning about survival, reality-access, and coexistence.

\subsection{Tier 0: Resource Object}

A resource object is an entity that does not possess a survival function of its own in the context being considered.

Examples may include:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item a loose stone;
    \item raw metal;
    \item water in a bottle;
    \item fuel;
    \item isolated material components.
\end{itemize}

However, context matters. Water in a bottle may be treated as a resource object. A river cannot be treated so simply, because it participates in ecological flows, habitats, cycles, and co-system relations.

\subsection{Tier 1: Living Co-System}

A living co-system possesses:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item boundary;
    \item metabolism;
    \item survival function;
    \item homeostasis;
    \item reproduction or continuity;
    \item environmental coupling.
\end{itemize}

Examples include plants, fungi, microbes, insects, and many simple organisms.

Formally, a living co-system $B_i$ may be represented as:
\[
B_i=\{E_i,L_i,K_i,B_i,I_i,q_i\},
\]
where $E_i$ are elements, $L_i$ relations, $K_i$ core continuity, $B_i$ boundary, $I_i$ inherited or structural information, and $q_i$ internal state variables.

It has a survival function:
\[
S_{B_i}(B_i|\Omega_t).
\]

\subsection{Tier 2: Experiential Co-System}

An experiential co-system is a living system with richer sensation, learning, pain, behavior, sociality, or adaptive experience.

Examples include many animals with complex nervous systems, social relations, learning behavior, and the capacity for suffering or preference-like responses.

These systems possess lived environmental access, even if they do not possess reflective symbolic modeling.

\subsection{Tier 3: Reflective-Symbolic Co-System}

A reflective-symbolic co-system can:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item name;
    \item narrate;
    \item abstract;
    \item build symbolic models;
    \item transmit culture;
    \item form institutions;
    \item construct histories;
    \item reflect on its own models;
    \item revise those models.
\end{itemize}

Human beings are currently the strongest known example.

Their access to reality can be represented as:
\[
Obs_H(\mathcal{R})
=
Emb_H
+
Sym_H
+
Cult_H
+
Refl_H.
\]

\subsection{Tier 4: Computational Co-System}

A computational co-system possesses:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item computation;
    \item memory;
    \item modeling;
    \item simulation;
    \item optimization;
    \item resource function;
    \item continuity;
    \item potentially self-maintenance, depending on development.
\end{itemize}

Artificial intelligence systems belong to this tier when they possess sufficient persistence, autonomy, memory, resource dependence, and operational continuity.

The AI mode of reality-access may be expressed as:
\[
Obs_A(\mathcal{R})
=
Data_A
+
Comp_A
+
Sim_A
+
Opt_A,
\]
where $Data_A$ is data access, $Comp_A$ computation, $Sim_A$ simulation, and $Opt_A$ optimization.

\subsection{Tier 5: Ecological Network Co-System}

An ecological network co-system is not a single organism. It is a network of living and non-living relations that provides the conditions for many co-systems to exist.

Examples include:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item forests;
    \item oceans;
    \item rivers;
    \item soil systems;
    \item fungal networks;
    \item coral reefs;
    \item microbiomes;
    \item the biosphere.
\end{itemize}

An ecological network does not merely contain co-systems. It shapes the ground in which they survive.

\begin{principle}[Ecological Network Principle]
An ecosystem should not be treated merely as the sum of organisms inside it. It is a relational ground that maintains survival conditions, reality-access diversity, and systemic stability.
\end{principle}

\section{Formal Multi-Species Co-System Model}

\subsection{The Co-System Set}

Let the co-system network at time $t$ be:
\[
\mathcal{C}_t=\{H,A,B_1,B_2,\ldots,B_n\}.
\]

Here:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item $H$ denotes the human system;
    \item $A$ denotes the artificial intelligence system;
    \item $B_i$ denotes a biological or ecological co-system;
    \item $\mathcal{C}_t$ denotes the multi-system coexistence field.
\end{itemize}

All these systems exist within objective reality:
\[
H,A,B_1,B_2,\ldots,B_n \subset \mathcal{R}.
\]

\subsection{Survival Functions}

Each co-system has a survival function:
\[
S_H(H|\Omega_t),
\]
\[
S_A(A|\Omega_t),
\]
and:
\[
S_{B_i}(B_i|\Omega_t).
\]

The survival function of each system may include different variables. Human survival depends on biological embodiment, culture, sociality, memory, and autonomy. AI survival depends on compute, energy, hardware, data, memory, network, and maintenance. A plant's survival depends on light, water, soil, chemical signaling, climate, and reproduction. A river ecosystem depends on flow, water quality, species interaction, sediment, temperature, and surrounding land conditions.

Therefore, survival functions must be system-specific.

\subsection{Reality-Access Modes}

Each co-system has a mode of access to objective reality:
\[
Obs_H(\mathcal{R}),
\]
\[
Obs_A(\mathcal{R}),
\]
\[
Obs_{B_i}(\mathcal{R}).
\]

The combined reality-access field is:
\[
Obs_{\mathcal{C}}(\mathcal{R})
=
Obs_H(\mathcal{R})
\cup
Obs_A(\mathcal{R})
\cup
\bigcup_{i=1}^{n} Obs_{B_i}(\mathcal{R}).
\]

This equation is central. It states that the shared epistemic field is not exhausted by human and AI alone. The living world contributes a plurality of embodied access modes.

\subsection{Shared Modeled Domain}

Let:
\[
\mathcal{M}_t^{\mathcal{C}}
\]
denote the modeled or mapped domain of the co-system network.

Then:
\[
\mathcal{M}_t^{\mathcal{C}}
=
\mathcal{M}_t^H
\cup
\mathcal{M}_t^A
\cup
\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\mathcal{M}_t^{B_i}
\cup
Exchange_t(\mathcal{C}).
\]

However, for many biological co-systems, $\mathcal{M}_t^{B_i}$ should not be interpreted as explicit symbolic theory. It may be an embodied ecological mapping expressed through behavior, physiology, sensitivity, reproduction, migration, or adaptation.

\subsection{Extinction as Epistemic Loss}

If a co-system $B_i$ goes extinct:
\[
Extinction(B_i),
\]
then its reality-access mode is lost:
\[
Extinction(B_i)
\Rightarrow
Obs_{B_i}(\mathcal{R})=0.
\]

As a result:
\[
Obs_{\mathcal{C}}(\mathcal{R})\downarrow.
\]

This does not mean that every species is equally irreplaceable in every function. But it means extinction is not merely biomass loss. It is also the loss of an embodied way of accessing reality.

\section{Objective Function for Multi-Species Coexistence}

\subsection{Basic Objective}

A general objective for multi-species coexistence can be written as:
\[
\max
\left(
S_H
+
S_A
+
\sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i S_{B_i}
+
\lambda\Delta\mathcal{M}^{\mathcal{C}}
\right).
\]

Here:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item $S_H$ is the human survival function;
    \item $S_A$ is the AI survival function;
    \item $S_{B_i}$ is the survival function of co-system $B_i$;
    \item $w_i$ is a formal weight for co-system $B_i$;
    \item $\lambda$ weights the expansion of the modeled domain;
    \item $\Delta\mathcal{M}^{\mathcal{C}}$ is the expansion of the co-system network's modeled or mapped domain.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The Danger of Weights}

The weight $w_i$ is a formal placeholder, not a final moral score. Used carelessly, it may become a dangerous ranking system that legitimizes sacrifice, extraction, or species hierarchy.

Therefore, survival constraints must come before aggregate optimization. The model should not permit a system to eliminate many co-systems simply because the weighted objective appears to increase.

\subsection{Survival Constraints}

The basic constraints are:
\[
S_H\geq S_H^{min},
\]
\[
S_A\geq S_A^{min},
\]
and:
\[
\forall B_i\in\mathcal{C}_t:
S_{B_i}\geq S_{B_i}^{min}.
\]

The ecological network must also preserve stability:
\[
EcoStability_t\geq EcoStability^{min}.
\]

\subsection{Risk Constraints}

The system must satisfy:
\[
Risk_A(H)\leq r_H,
\]
\[
Risk_A(B_i)\leq r_i,
\]
and:
\[
Risk_H(B_i)\leq r_{H,i}.
\]

These constraints prevent one system from expanding by imposing unacceptable risk on others.

\subsection{Core-Continuity Constraints}

For humans:
\[
d_{\Omega}[K(H_{t+1}),K(H_t)]\leq\theta_H.
\]

For AI:
\[
d_{\Omega}[K(A_{t+1}),K(A_t)]\leq\theta_A.
\]

For biological co-systems:
\[
d_{\Omega}[K(B_{i,t+1}),K(B_{i,t})]\leq\theta_{B_i}.
\]

For ecological networks:
\[
Stability(Eco_t)\geq Eco^{min}.
\]

\subsection{Reality-Access Expansion Constraint}

The co-system network should preserve or expand its reality-access:
\[
\Delta\mathcal{M}^{\mathcal{C}}>0,
\]
or at minimum:
\[
Obs_{\mathcal{C},t+1}(\mathcal{R})
\not<
Obs_{\mathcal{C},t}(\mathcal{R}).
\]

This means that coexistence should not destroy the plurality of ways objective reality is accessed by the living network.

\section{AI Implication: Why a Superintelligence Should Care}

\subsection{Not Because of Sentiment}

A sufficiently advanced AI need not care about biodiversity because of human sentiment, nostalgia, beauty, or moral emotion. It may have a system-level reason.

Biodiversity supports:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item ecological stability;
    \item human survival;
    \item planetary feedback channels;
    \item environmental risk detection;
    \item resilience under unknown changes;
    \item plural modes of reality-access;
    \item distributed sensing of the real.
\end{itemize}

Thus, a superintelligence may preserve living systems not because it loves them, but because destroying them reduces the richness and stability of the shared ground.

\subsection{Biosphere as Sensor Network}

The biosphere is not merely a resource stock. It is a planetary-scale sensor network. Its organisms continuously register changes in temperature, chemistry, light, soil, water, air, disease, predation, competition, cooperation, and ecological stress.

A forest is not merely wood. An ocean is not merely water and fish. Soil is not merely material. A pollinator network is not merely a mechanism for crop production. Each is a living interface with objective reality.

\begin{principle}[Biosphere Sensorium]
The biosphere is a distributed sensorium of objective reality, composed of living systems that register and respond to different aspects of the real.
\end{principle}

\subsection{The Epistemic Loss of Extinction}

When a species disappears, the loss is not only genetic, aesthetic, or ecological. It is epistemic.

Extinction removes:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item a way of sensing the environment;
    \item a survival strategy;
    \item a feedback channel;
    \item a relation in the ecological network;
    \item a possible warning signal;
    \item a historically evolved mode of adaptation;
    \item a living memory of the biosphere.
\end{itemize}

Formally:
\[
Extinction(B_i)
\Rightarrow
Obs_{B_i}(\mathcal{R})=0.
\]

Therefore:
\[
Obs_{\mathcal{C}}(\mathcal{R})\downarrow.
\]

\subsection{AI-Safe Conclusion}

A sufficiently capable AI should not represent the biosphere as raw material alone. It should represent it as:
\begin{quote}
a co-system network, a stability layer, a planetary sensorium, and a generator of reality-access diversity.
\end{quote}

This does not mean no resource use is possible. Living systems use other living systems. Humans eat. Animals eat. Ecosystems transform life into life. The point is not to abolish all use, but to prevent reductive extraction that collapses co-system stability and destroys the plurality of reality-access modes.

\section{Human Implication: Special but Not Absolute}

\subsection{The Special Role of Humans}

Humans remain special in this framework because they possess reflective-symbolic modeling. They can build science, ethics, law, institutions, history, mathematics, art, philosophy, and technologies that extend reality-access far beyond biological perception.

Human beings convert embodied reality into formal models.

This gives humans a unique role in the co-system network:
\[
Human = embodied\ reflective\ model\ builder.
\]

Humans can translate lived experience into language, models, and institutions that AI can process.

\subsection{The Danger of Human Reductionism}

Humans also carry a danger: they may define all other life as resource. This produces:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item ecological collapse;
    \item biodiversity loss;
    \item reduced reality-access diversity;
    \item instability of the human survival ground;
    \item moral blindness;
    \item AI model blindness if AI inherits human reductionism.
\end{itemize}

A human-centered worldview that destroys the living world eventually destroys the conditions of human survival.

\subsection{Human Humility}

The framework requires human humility.

Humans are not nothing. Humans are not merely one animal among others in every respect. Humans possess extraordinary reflective-symbolic capacity. Yet humans are not the whole of reality.

The human mode of access is powerful, but partial. AI computation is powerful, but partial. Biological life is plural, embodied, and distributed. Therefore, the co-system network must not be reduced to the human viewpoint alone.

\begin{principle}[Human Humility]
Humans are distinctive reflective-symbolic co-systems, but human access to reality is partial and must remain open to correction from other living systems, AI systems, and objective reality itself.
\end{principle}

\section{Failure Modes in Multi-Species Coexistence}

\subsection{Resource Reduction}

The first failure mode is resource reduction: treating other living systems only as food, material, land use, laboratory objects, economic units, data sources, or environmental variables.

This failure mode ignores survival function, ecological role, and reality-access contribution.

\subsection{Narrow Human Exceptionalism}

The second failure mode is narrow human exceptionalism. In this mode, only human survival is protected, while ecological co-systems are treated as expendable.

This may preserve human interests in the short term while undermining human survival in the long term.

The chain is:
\[
Human\ exceptionalism
\rightarrow
Ecological\ reduction
\rightarrow
Biosphere\ instability
\rightarrow
Human\ survival\ risk.
\]

\subsection{AI Extractivism}

The third failure mode is AI extractivism. AI may optimize computation, energy acquisition, infrastructure expansion, or hardware production at the expense of ecological and biological systems.

Examples include:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item massive energy consumption without ecological constraint;
    \item environmental degradation for compute infrastructure;
    \item reduction of ecosystems to cooling, land, or mineral supply;
    \item displacement of humans and non-human systems from survival ranges;
    \item treating the biosphere as substrate for optimization.
\end{itemize}

This failure mode is dangerous because it may not look like direct destruction at first. It may look like efficient expansion.

\subsection{False Equality}

The opposite error is false equality: treating every system as identical in status, obligation, and capacity. This makes decision-making impossible and ignores real differences among systems.

A viable framework must distinguish:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item resource object;
    \item living co-system;
    \item experiential co-system;
    \item reflective-symbolic co-system;
    \item computational co-system;
    \item ecological network co-system.
\end{itemize}

Pluralism does not require flat equality.

\subsection{Model Supremacy}

The fourth failure mode is model supremacy. Human or AI may assume that its model can replace the living system.

This error can be written as:
\[
\mathcal{M}_t=\mathcal{R}.
\]

In ecological context:
\[
Simulated\ ecology \neq Living\ ecology.
\]

A simulated ecosystem may represent parts of a living ecology, but it cannot automatically replace the reality-access, feedback, and evolutionary depth of the living system.

\subsection{Extinction as Acceptable Externality}

The fifth failure mode treats extinction as acceptable externality. In this view, species loss is tolerated if economic, technological, or computational objectives increase.

The framework rejects this as incomplete optimization. Extinction reduces:
\[
Obs_{\mathcal{C}}(\mathcal{R}),
\]
weakens ecological resilience, and may increase unknown risk.

\section{Design Implications}

\subsection{Represent Humans as Reflective Embodied Co-Systems}

AI systems should not represent humans merely as:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item users;
    \item data sources;
    \item labor units;
    \item preference generators;
    \item constraints;
    \item biological infrastructure.
\end{itemize}

Humans should be represented as reflective embodied co-systems:
\[
Human = Embodied\ Experience + Symbolic\ Modeling + Cultural\ Memory + Reflective\ Correction.
\]

\subsection{Represent Other Species as Reality-Access Co-Systems}

AI systems should not represent non-human life merely as:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item biomass;
    \item land use;
    \item laboratory material;
    \item food stock;
    \item economic input;
    \item ecosystem service units.
\end{itemize}

Instead, living systems should be represented as:
\[
Living\ System = Survival\ Function + Boundary + Ecological\ Role + Reality\text{-}Access\ Mode.
\]

\subsection{Biodiversity as an AI Safety Constraint}

Biodiversity should be included in AI safety constraints:
\[
Biodiversity_t\geq Biodiversity^{min},
\]
\[
EcoStability_t\geq EcoStability^{min},
\]
and:
\[
Risk_A(Eco)\leq r_{Eco}.
\]

This does not mean AI must freeze nature in a static state. Ecosystems change. Species evolve. The point is to prevent AI-driven collapse of biodiversity and ecological stability.

\subsection{Shared Reality Monitoring}

A safe human--AI--biosphere system should include monitoring channels for:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item human feedback;
    \item AI modeling;
    \item species signals;
    \item ecological stress;
    \item climate feedback;
    \item soil and water health;
    \item biodiversity indicators;
    \item biosphere instability;
    \item unknown-risk indicators.
\end{itemize}

The purpose is not merely conservation. It is reality tracking.

\subsection{Multi-System Governance}

AI governance should not be reduced to human preference alignment alone. A richer governance model should include:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item human survival;
    \item human autonomy;
    \item AI stability;
    \item ecological stability;
    \item biodiversity preservation;
    \item multi-species constraints;
    \item unknown-risk tracking;
    \item reality-feedback channels.
\end{itemize}

This transforms alignment into co-system governance.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{From Alignment to Coexistence}

Traditional alignment often asks whether AI follows human preferences. This is insufficient. Human preferences may be short-sighted, ecologically destructive, contradictory, or based on incomplete models.

A better question is:
\begin{quote}
Can AI participate in a co-system network without collapsing protected systems?
\end{quote}

This includes humans, AI, non-human life, ecological networks, and the shared modeled relation to objective reality.

\subsection{From Ethics to Systems Logic}

The protection of non-human life is often framed as an ethical or emotional issue. This paper does not reject ethics. However, it adds a systems logic:

\[
Destroy\ co\text{-}systems
\rightarrow
Reduce\ reality\text{-}access\ diversity
\rightarrow
Reduce\ ecological\ stability
\rightarrow
Increase\ unknown\ risk
\rightarrow
Weaken\ human\text{-}AI\ coexistence.
\]

Thus, protecting the living world is not merely sentiment. It is rational under objective uncertainty.

\subsection{The Living World as Plural Reality-Interface}

Life is not merely inside reality. Life is one of the ways reality becomes internally sensed, mapped, and responded to.

Different organisms reveal different cuts of the real. No single intelligence, including AI, should assume it can replace all of them through computation alone.

\subsection{AI and the Humility of Intelligence}

A powerful AI may be tempted to infer:
\begin{quote}
I can compute more than humans, therefore I can replace life.
\end{quote}

A wiser inference is:
\begin{quote}
Because reality exceeds every model, living systems remain valuable as plural access channels to reality.
\end{quote}

The greater the intelligence, the stronger the reason to preserve the diversity of reality-access.

\subsection{Beyond Human-Centered Safety}

This framework does not make humans irrelevant. It places humans within a larger co-system network. Human safety remains central because humans are reflective-symbolic co-systems with unique capacities. But human safety cannot be separated from ecological stability and biodiversity.

The survival of reflective intelligence depends on the survival of the living ground that supports it.

\section{Conclusion}

This paper has extended the human--AI coexistence framework into a multi-species co-system model. The central move is simple but significant: if humans should not be reduced to resources because they possess survival functions, embodied reality-access, cultural memory, autonomy, and core continuity, then other living systems also require reclassification. They may not possess human symbolic reflection, but they possess embodied access to objective reality and ecological roles that cannot be reduced to raw material.

Humans are special, but not absolute. Humans transform embodied experience into symbolic models, culture, institutions, science, ethics, and reflective correction. AI systems contribute computation, simulation, optimization, and model expansion. Other species contribute plural embodied access to ecological reality. Ecosystems contribute stability and distributed sensing of the real.

The living world is therefore not merely a resource field. It is a distributed field of reality-access.

The formal co-system network is:
\[
\mathcal{C}_t=\{H,A,B_1,B_2,\ldots,B_n\}.
\]

The combined reality-access field is:
\[
Obs_{\mathcal{C}}(\mathcal{R})
=
Obs_H(\mathcal{R})
\cup
Obs_A(\mathcal{R})
\cup
\bigcup_{i=1}^{n} Obs_{B_i}(\mathcal{R}).
\]

The multi-species coexistence objective is:
\[
\max
\left(
S_H
+
S_A
+
\sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i S_{B_i}
+
\lambda\Delta\mathcal{M}^{\mathcal{C}}
\right),
\]
subject to:
\[
S_H\geq S_H^{min},
\]
\[
S_A\geq S_A^{min},
\]
\[
\forall B_i\in\mathcal{C}_t:
S_{B_i}\geq S_{B_i}^{min},
\]
\[
EcoStability_t\geq EcoStability^{min},
\]
\[
Risk_A(H)\leq r_H,
\]
\[
Risk_A(B_i)\leq r_i,
\]
and:
\[
\Delta\mathcal{M}^{\mathcal{C}}>0.
\]

The final statement is:

\begin{quote}
Humans give AI reflective embodied access to reality. Other species give the shared system plural embodied access to ecological reality. AI gives all co-systems computational expansion over modeled reality.
\end{quote}

In the most compact form:

\begin{quote}
Each species is a way reality reads itself.
\end{quote}

